#Hall of the Celestial Kings

ICON₁ — Maitreya (布袋彌勒佛, Budai form) 
POSITION    : central, axial
SCALE       : intermediate
              (subordinate to CELESTIAL_KINGS,
               dominant over SKANDA)
ORIENTATION : outward-facing (toward front entrance)
FUNCTION    : soteriological, devotional, cosmological

ICON₂ — Skanda (韋馱菩薩, Wei Tuo) 
POSITION    : axial, recessed (immediately behind MAITREYA)
SCALE       : subordinate
ORIENTATION : outward-facing (toward back entrance)
FUNCTION    : apotropaic, protective

ICON₃ — King Chíguó (持國天王, Dhr̥tarāṣṭra) + King Zēngzhǎng (增長天王, Virūḍhaka) (Right side of hall)
POSITION    : peripheral, paired-lateral
SCALE       : dominant
ORIENTATION : inward-facing
FUNCTION    : apotropaic, protective, cosmological, administrative

ICON₄ — King Guǎngmù (廣目天王, Virūpākṣa) + King Duōwén (多聞天王, Vaiśravaṇa) (Left side of hall)
POSITION    : peripheral, paired-lateral
SCALE       : dominant
ORIENTATION : inward-facing
FUNCTION    : apotropaic, protective, cosmological, administrative
The Four Celestial Kings (Sì Dà Tiānwáng 四大天王) are the guardians of the four cardinal directions in Buddhist cosmology, dwelling on the slopes of Mount Meru and protecting the world from demonic incursion. Their iconographic attributes are diagnostic: Dhr̥tarāṣṭra holds a pipa lute (his music subdues demons); Virūḍhaka holds a sword; Virūpākṣa holds a serpent and a pearl; Vaiśravaṇa holds a pagoda and a banner. Their paired arrangement — two per side wall — divides the canonical group of four into two facing groups of two, each group constituting an altar ensemble within the larger hall ensemble.
Their dominant scale relative to Maitreya is one of the most analytically significant features of this hall and a direct test of interpretation rules R1 and R2, discussed below.
Their administrative function is important to record explicitly: the Four Celestial Kings are not merely protective figures — they hold offices within the Buddhist cosmic hierarchy, governing the four directions and reporting on human conduct to Indra (Śakra). Their presence in this hall encodes the administrative structure of the Buddhist cosmos, not only its defensive perimeter.
